  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe local area resides between high pressure located north of \nthe Great Lakes and low pressure over the Ohio Valley. Shallow \ncold advection continues this morning with northeasterly surface\nwinds. While the heavier area of rain is exiting to the \nsoutheast, scattered showers continue in an arc from near Marion\nto Akron where lift continues along a tightly packed baroclinic\nzone at 850mb. This boundary remains in place through the day \nand lift is likely to increase through early afternoon as a \nshortwave passes aloft. Will continue with 60-70% pops today \nalong this axis with additional rainfall generally around a \nquarter inch. Dry air continues to filter into northeast \nportions of the forecast area where pops will be low or dry \ntowards the Ashtabula/Erie lakeshore. A brisk northeasterly flow\nwill continue off Lake Erie with lakeshore areas and portions \nof Northwest Ohio seeing winds of 10-15 mph with gusts to 25+ \nmph.\n\nWill continue with scattered showers into this evening with lift \nlocated to the north of an inverted surface trough that extends \nnorth up the Ohio/Pennsylvania border. Eventually mid-level dry air \narrives and much of the area will be dry on Tuesday. Low chances of \nshowers are focused downwind of Lake Erie in Northeast Ohio where \ngood low level convergence and some lake enhancement may maintain \nscattered light showers. Will also allow for low 20-40 pops in the \neast where lift continues along that lingering frontal boundary in \nthe 850-700mb layer. \n\nBeginning late Tuesday night and continuing through Wednesday pops \nramp up across central and eastern portions of the forecast area as \nan upper level trough arrives with a return of deeper moisture. The \n00Z NAM is an outlier with the upper trough a little farther south \nand is therefore drier. Will lean the forecast towards the better \nmodel agreement which keeps scattered to numerous showers in our \nforecast area. With the better coverage of clouds and showers, \nlowered high temperatures by a degree or two across our interior \ncounties where pops are greater than 60%. High temperatures will \nremain solidly in the 60s with the cool northeast flow and cloudy \nconditions. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe upper level trough moves southeast to the Mid-\nAtlantic on Thursday with surface high pressure strengthening across \nthe eastern Great Lakes. Drier air arrives bringing an end to \nprecipitation with gradual clearing. The cool and dry airmass will \nsupport the coolest lows we have seen so far this season beginning \non Thursday night with inland lows solidly in the 40s with low 40s \nfor interior portions of Nw Pennsylvania. This will repeat Friday \nnight before trending just slightly warmer for Saturday night. Some \nof this will depend on winds and ability to decouple but can \ncertainly expect a chill in the air.",
